{:edb/db-before-t 1560 :edb/db-after-t 1561 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534873 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:44:51.959Z" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119564 1100 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}$. Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139534873 false] [17592186119564 1100 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139534873 true] [17592186119564 1145 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= p + tv \\\\\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534873 false] [17592186119564 1145 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119565 1100 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=-2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139534873 true] [17592186119565 1100 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n${x = 1 - 2t; y = 4t; z =-2 + 3t$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$. Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139534873 false] [17592186119565 1145 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 - 2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2 + 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nr &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}\n$$" 13194139534873 false] [17592186119565 1145 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119566 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119566 1100 "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1799/Source/Images/d43fa9b119feb6bcf4031525817fc87f.png)" 13194139534873 false] [17592186119566 1145 "We know that the line passes through the points\n\n$$\nP\\left(-1,1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ\\left(5,-1, 0\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPQ^(→) &= ⟨5,-1, 0⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨6,-2, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-(-1)}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z-0}{0} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component is always maintains the constant value $z=0$. For example, our two points\n\n$$\nP\\left(-1,1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ\\left(5,-1,0\\right)\n$$\n\nboth have $z$-coordinates of $z=0$.\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{z}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $z=0$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{6}=\\dfrac{y-1}{-2},\\; z=0\n$$" 13194139534873 false] [17592186119566 1145 "We know that the line passes through the points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/bf6bd67f4c98a8d36673d51a9fd9ee42ce53c45dcd793e19b36796908be0dfca.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/80d08830e9c7790842989560740c1a8c09a2cf794399799fa44f0bfd302f165a.png) Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/54f43a22493f78d42649844a6e360d5226cc8cb7c19af11c8e8b595354063789.png) which is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/9eec5064637ab51b30b8bcae7d33babac63edd10da36315ab18c61da76f7b102.png)\n\nUsing the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/dee4f9a47a1bdff7b5bee4b328768e8967ff52dba70800b6defa0575a7034847.png), we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fdf49c280c1dcb705153d75b3f52527d7a3d6e5be88fc2497d69553ea9de953b.png)\nor equivalently\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/d606ca3e74d93f6a3e4d802603a1b27729301cd7b9611ed94d0514a6a2997f4b.png)\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/7153fc0366e9f632513e320313657591fe8f5a4238f1f0987ada184183e462ac.png) involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/d0a37c7a07a4a5e017e3edb659fad8defff4fa33a3accbd0e6fd2d6d8439cf94.png) component always maintains the constant value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/b7f9100157159abea8f5f7a86a272d5a539e2d8084e8c894642bc49342e2a76f.png) For example, our two points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/ad5d9528b6a85fa9d0e010a6c7b0fe7d51253b71fe995a2010204708256aa081.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/5a762bf2a30c7fb3be3ba2e1d8abf1b308614d123e11600d93d7b3b02fe828c7.png) both have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fdc688a7d7010425aace3db801008b0b83b950e7ff7c03a89f9745d4651e1dca.png)-coordinates of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/9626663f5000cd7c8c37eeec3c498de49124f3cc6807fcaf713b85e6a692a595.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/7ff8eda495247902c9dfd90af00e4544a0090cab531689e7b7a833bce66fd9ac.png) part and instead write ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/f0d48b15791c63393dea1717a24819d4ba7502f28b54a363f5c9890b0bdb1079.png) as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/41d29b651c736cf13b6cbde92ffc46ae33bfb3e139ebec8aff2f7c05f431fde7.png)" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119583 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/fea24c391122278ffe20ec0f6a7d704b835aca18c3bdcd7395aeeea0f593a2bf.png)" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119583 1100 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534873 false] [17592186119583 1145 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/368f42200b9366547bdff9840a894b63d3df43722052ecb1a4e15e2306b4e56a.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186119583 1145 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\ninvolves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 + 4t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 + 5t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-2}{0}\n$$\n\npart and instead write $y=2$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139534873 false] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316432 13194139534873 true] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316439 13194139534873 true] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316488 13194139534873 true] [17592186119750 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316515 13194139534873 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316446 13194139534873 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316453 13194139534873 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316501 13194139534873 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316522 13194139534873 true] [17592186119751 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316529 13194139534873 true] [17592186119752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316474 13194139534873 true] [17592186119752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316481 13194139534873 true] [17592186119752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316508 13194139534873 true] [17592186119752 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316536 13194139534873 true] [17592186119766 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316460 13194139534873 true] [17592186119766 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316467 13194139534873 true] [17592186119766 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316543 13194139534873 true] [17592186119766 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316550 13194139534873 true] [17592186119766 1020 #edb/ref 17592186316557 13194139534873 true] [17592186120153 1017 #edb/ref 17592186117438 13194139534873 false] [17592186120153 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316495 13194139534873 true] [17592186120153 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534873 true] [17592186120153 1100 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x}{1} = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = \\frac{z}{3}$. Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139534873 false] [17592186120153 1100 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139534873 true] [17592186120153 1145 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-1,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,2,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186120176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534873 true] [17592186120176 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/assets/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139534873 true] [17592186120176 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1799/Source/Images/q-33234.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." 13194139534873 false] [17592186120176 1145 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,3,1)$ and $Q(5,-1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,4⟩-⟨-2,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316432 1017 #edb/ref 17592186316433 13194139534873 true] [17592186316432 1089 "q-127068" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316432 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534873 true] [17592186316432 1100 "Find the vector equation of the line $\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z+3}{7}.$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316432 1113 #uuid "ef077b10-897a-59ae-a756-7a254900bdb1" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316432 1145 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{7}\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-2,7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316434 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316435 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316436 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316437 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1000 #edb/ref 17592186316438 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1041 "selection" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1104 #edb/ref 17592186316436 13194139534873 true] [17592186316433 1110 #uuid "793741a6-9263-5d51-9444-560559743e11" 13194139534873 true] [17592186316434 1034 "r=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -7\\end{a